/T (Both counts are 20: after the DELETE the new passenger is gone, and after ROLLBACK the original data is restored \(flight 113 is Delayed again and the pending fares are back to normal\). Deleting a passenger who has bookings, for example DELETE FROM Passenger WHERE Passenger_ID = 1;, would be rejected by the foreign key with error 1451, as shown in the data integrity tests.)
/E (Both counts are 20: after the DELETE the new passenger is gone, and after ROLLBACK the original data is restored \(flight 113 is Delayed again and the pending fares are back to normal\). Deleting a passenger who has bookings, for example DELETE FROM Passenger WHERE Passenger_ID = 1;, would be rejected by the foreign key with error 1451, as shown in the data integrity tests.)
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
662 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 142 0 R
/T (The queries show how SQL retrieves exactly the data the airline needs. WHERE selects rows, ORDER BY arranges them, aggregate functions summarize them, GROUP BY summarizes per category \(status, payment method, seat class, aircraft\), and HAVING keeps only the groups that meet a condition, such as flights with at least four bookings or routes with a high average fare. Combined with joins, these clauses turn the nine related tables into answers about revenue, load, customer behaviour and fleet use.)
/E (The queries show how SQL retrieves exactly the data the airline needs. WHERE selects rows, ORDER BY arranges them, aggregate functions summarize them, GROUP BY summarizes per category \(status, payment method, seat class, aircraft\), and HAVING keeps only the groups that meet a condition, such as flights with at least four bookings or routes with a high average fare. Combined with joins, these clauses turn the nine related tables into answers about revenue, load, customer behaviour and fleet use.)
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
